Authoritative cumulative snapshot of ref/linux-7.1.5: everything the
cooperative guest needs, as one diff against the released tree.

Generated against download/linux-7.1.5.tar.xz on 2026-08-07, and verified
by reverse-applying it to ref/linux-7.1.5 with no rejects.

Over the previous snapshot:

  * SMP rung S2: an SMP=y guest, still on one processor, with the local APIC
    never touched.

    co_colinux_vcpu_id() is implemented for CONFIG_SMP=y as
    raw_cpu_read(cpu_number). cpu_number is declared in asm/irqflags.h rather
    than reached through <asm/smp.h>, which that header cannot include without
    circularity. Not __this_cpu_read: it expands to a preempt check that is
    static under CONFIG_DEBUG_PREEMPT, and native_save_fl() is a non-static
    inline, so it does not even compile -- and these helpers are noinstr and
    want no instrumentation regardless.

    GS is valid before any C code runs, which is the part that had to be
    checked rather than assumed. The guest enters at co_arch_start_kernel and
    falls into common_startup_64, which writes MSR_GS_BASE from
    __per_cpu_offset[0]. Until setup_per_cpu_areas() the boot processor reads
    its per-CPU variables out of the init image, where cpu_number is 0, and
    afterwards out of its own area, where setup_per_cpu_areas() wrote 0. There
    is no window in which the boot processor gets the wrong answer.

  * Two topology exemptions, and the reasoning is the whole point.

    The kernel forces possible_cpus to 1 when the local APIC is disabled, in
    topology_apply_cmdline_limits_early() and again in restrict_to_up(). That
    encodes a true fact about real hardware -- without a local APIC there is
    no way to INIT-SIPI another processor, so there can only be one -- and it
    is not true here. This guest's processors are started by the host, each
    one a pinned host thread entering through the world switch with a complete
    initial state, and they signal each other through shared memory. XEN PV is
    already exempted from restrict_to_up() with a comment describing the same
    situation; a cooperative guest is exempted alongside it.

    So nolapic STAYS on the command line. That is the safe direction: the APIC
    is the host's chip, driving the host's timer, keyboard and disk, and
    nolapic is how this port keeps the guest's hands off it. What changes is
    only the inference drawn from it, not the fact.

  * smp_ops, replacing two native operations that would reach real hardware.

    native_smp_prepare_cpus() switches on apic_intr_mode and, with no usable
    APIC, answers by calling disable_smp() -- correct for the hardware it
    describes, wrong for this machine. co_colinux_smp_prepare_cpus() does the
    common preparation and nothing else: no local APIC timer, no UV, no
    INIT-SIPI delay calibration, no SEV-SNP wakeup.

    co_colinux_kick_ap() refuses, loudly, and is a safety belt rather than a
    placeholder. The native version reaches do_boot_cpu(), which sends
    INIT-SIPI-SIPI to a real physical processor of the machine this guest is a
    thread on -- it would be trying to boot one of Windows' own CPUs into
    Linux. It earned its place on the first boot: the guest came up with three
    possible CPUs and the kernel duly tried to start CPUs 1 and 2.

    Measured on the M92p under Windows 10: "CPU topo: Allowing 3 present CPUs",
    nr_cpu_ids 3, "3 processor(s) possible", both AP attempts refused, "Brought
    up 1 node, 1 CPU", and the guest booted on to /sbin/init exactly as the
    uniprocessor build does. co_colinux_adopt_traps() also runs clean under
    SMP=y, which answers the last open question about it.

